# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""
Six-dataset version of the revised manuscript (three human and three mouse datasets per immune cell type, each with
at least three replicates). Human-mouse classification = DSA option 1 with the requirement: one junction per event;
unchanged = ANOVA over the six datasets not significant and dataset PSIs within 0.1; differentially spliced = ANOVA
adjusted P <= 0.05, Wilcoxon all human vs all mouse samples adjusted P <= 0.05, and 2 x the largest within-species
|dPSI| < the smallest human-mouse |dPSI| (dataset PSIs).
Numbers: ensembl116/revision_6ds/manuscript_numbers.md (facts_116.py), events/id50_6ds_anova/.
Applied at the end of revised_text_116.py, after text_anova_updates.py.
"""

UPDATES6 = {
    8: ('We mapped exons and alternative splicing events between human and mouse, and compared the use ratio of 6,784 '
        'orthologous alternative splicing events between three human and three mouse RNA-sequencing datasets of each of'
        ' five immune cell types, and between human and mouse fibroblasts.'),
    9: ('Of the 2,509-2,972 alternative splicing events that were testable in each immune cell type, 5.7-7.8% were '
        'splicing conserved, that is, the use ratio did not differ between the six datasets (one-way ANOVA P > 0.1) and'
        ' varied by at most 0.05 across them; almost all of them (99%) are events in which one alternative splicing '
        'outcome accounted for more than 95% of the reads. In 5.2-7.8% of the events, the mean use ratio differed '
        'between the human and the mouse datasets by at least 0.1, and every difference between a human and a mouse '
        'dataset was more than 1.5 times as large as every difference between two datasets of the same species. The '
        'other 85-89% of the events were inconclusive, mostly because datasets of the same species differed too much. '
        'Of the 459 events that were differentially spliced in at least one immune cell type and testable in '
        'fibroblasts, 48% were also differentially spliced between human and mouse fibroblasts.'),
    10: ('Our results show that, among testable orthologous alternative splicing events, a conserved use ratio between '
        'human and mouse immune cells can be demonstrated mostly for events dominated by one isoform, that for a few '
        'percent of the events the use ratio differs between species more than between datasets of the same species, '
        'and that these robust differences are frequently shared with fibroblasts. For most events, the differences '
        'between datasets of the same species are too large to determine whether the use ratio is conserved. By '
        'integrating exon orthology with observed splicing patterns, our analysis provides a resource for evaluating '
        'for which genes the mouse model recapitulates the alternative splicing ratio of human, and for which genes a '
        'different model should be sought.'),
    18: ('Since the prevalence of species-specific alternative splicing between human and mouse remains a controversial '
        'subject (29-31), we performed a systematic comparison of the use ratio of orthologous alternative splicing '
        'events between corresponding human and mouse immune cell types. Exon-level orthology (35), annotation-free '
        'quantification of local splicing events from junction reads (44) and cross-species comparison of splicing '
        'ratio (28-31) each have precedents; our contribution is their integration into an alternative splicing event '
        'orthology map, and applying differential splicing analysis of those events to sorted immune cell populations '
        'profiled in three independent datasets per species. There are 6,784 orthologous alternative splicing events in'
        ' which both alternatives are expressed in human and mouse immune cells. Out of the events that could be tested'
        ' in each cell type, 6-8% were splicing conserved, mostly events in which one isoform accounts for more than '
        '95% of the reads, whereas for 5-8% the use ratio differed between species by at least 0.1 and by more than '
        'every difference between datasets of the same species.'),
    29: ('To separate species differences from dataset differences, each of five immune cell types, B cells, CD4 T '
        'cells, CD8 T cells, natural killer (NK) cells and monocytes, was compared between three human and three mouse '
        'RNA-sequencing (RNA-seq) datasets (samples in Table S2). Public bulk RNA-seq datasets of sorted immune cells '
        'were searched in GEO (https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/) (36). Human datasets that profile several immune cell'
        ' types were searched with the query: (("B cells" AND "NK cells" AND monocytes) OR "immune cell types" OR '
        '"immune cell subsets" OR "haematopoietic cell types" OR "hematopoietic cell types") AND "Homo '
        'sapiens"[Organism] AND "expression profiling by high throughput sequencing"[DataSet Type] AND gse[Entry Type] '
        'AND ("7"[n_samples] : "100000"[n_samples]) AND ("2000/01/01"[PDAT] : "2026/10/19"[PDAT]), which returned 172 '
        'series. As no mouse dataset profiled the five cell types with at least three replicates of each, mouse '
        'datasets of the individual cell types were searched with the query: ("follicular B" OR "naive CD4" OR "naive '
        'CD8" OR "CD8 T cells" OR "NK cells" OR "Ly6Chi" OR "Ly6C+ monocytes") AND "Mus musculus"[Organism] AND '
        '"expression profiling by high throughput sequencing"[DataSet Type] AND gse[Entry Type] AND ("6"[n_samples] : '
        '"100000"[n_samples]) AND ("2000/01/01"[PDAT] : "2026/10/19"[PDAT]), which returned 2,805 series, and, for '
        'monocyte datasets that are not in GEO, in BioProject with the query: ("Ly6C" OR monocyte OR monocytes) AND '
        'txid10090[Organism:exp] AND ("2000/01/01"[Registration Date] : "2026/10/19"[Registration Date]), which '
        'returned 2,129 projects. For each candidate dataset, the sample records in GEO or BioSample and the run '
        'records in the Sequence Read Archive (SRA) (41) and the European Nucleotide Archive (ENA) were checked. The '
        'candidate datasets that were considered and the reason each was not used are listed in Table S3.'),
    37: ('Using the JSR expression table, differential splicing analysis was performed according to the workflow of '
        'LeafCutter (44) (https://davidaknowles.github.io/leafcutter/articles/Usage.html), separately for each cell '
        'type and each pair of datasets: the nine human-mouse pairs (H1, H2 or H3 vs M1, M2 or M3), the three human-'
        'human pairs (H1-H2, H2-H3, H1-H3) and the three mouse-mouse pairs (M1-M2, M1-M3, M2-M3; Figure S1A), and for '
        'human vs mouse fibroblasts. The human exon annotation of Ensembl release 116 was used to label the clusters '
        'with gene names. Default parameters were used, except for -g and -i. The -g parameter (min_samples_per_group) '
        'sets the minimal number of samples in each group that are covered by at least min_coverage reads. The -i '
        'parameter (min_samples_per_intron) sets the minimum number of samples in which an intron must have at least '
        'one supporting read to be retained; introns that do not meet this threshold are excluded. Both parameters are '
        'assigned a number which is 60% of the number of samples in the smallest group in the relevant comparison, and '
        'at least two (2-3, Tables S4, S5).'),
    38: ('The fraction of JSRs of a junction in each LeafCutter cluster in each sample is termed the percent spliced in '
        '(PSI). Differential splicing is measured in terms of the change in PSI between conditions, ΔPSI. Max(|ΔPSI|) '
        'was defined as the maximal abs(ΔPSI) value in each comparison, in each LeafCutter cluster, where the PSI of a '
        'group is the mean PSI over its samples with at least 20 JSRs in the cluster (the LeafCutter min_coverage).'),
    41: ('Based on the differential splicing analysis, in each comparison of two datasets of the same species, in the '
        'fibroblast comparison and in the sex, age and tissue comparisons (Supplementary Note 1), each alternative '
        'splicing event was assigned a status (Figure S1C): Not testable: fewer than the minimal number of samples (the'
        ' -g value) in a group with at least 20 JSRs; Differentially spliced: LeafCutter p.adjust ≤ 0.05 and '
        'max(|ΔPSI|) ≥ 0.1; Splicing conserved: LeafCutter P value > 0.1 and max(|ΔPSI|) ≤ 0.05; Inconclusive: a '
        'testable event that is neither differentially spliced nor splicing conserved. The p.adjust of the event is the'
        ' Benjamini-Hochberg adjusted P value reported by LeafCutter. The ΔPSI threshold of 0.1 is commonly used for '
        'splicing differences; a splicing conserved event requires both the absence of a difference at a lenient P '
        'value threshold and a difference of at most half that threshold, so that an event that is only not significant'
        ' is inconclusive rather than conserved. The human-mouse dataset pairs were analyzed by LeafCutter as well, but'
        ' only to estimate effect sizes (ΔPSI and PSI correlations), not to classify events.'),
    42: ('Human-mouse classification of the events in immune cells. To classify each event in each immune cell type '
        'between species, using all three human and all three mouse datasets together, the PSI of a dataset was defined'
        ' as the mean PSI over its samples with at least 20 JSRs in the cluster. As the PSI is calculated from all the '
        'reads of the event, a sample has either a PSI for all junctions of an event or for none, and an event was '
        'considered testable in a cell type if it had a PSI in at least two samples of each of the six datasets. '
        'Differences between the six datasets were tested by one-way analysis of variance (ANOVA) of the PSI of the '
        'samples, with the datasets as groups, implemented by the Python scipy.stats.f_oneway function, as in (59), on '
        'the junction with the largest range of dataset PSIs (maximum minus minimum over the six datasets, without '
        'using the species of the datasets). The testable events were classified as follows (Figure 1B). Splicing '
        'conserved: the ANOVA P value > 0.1, and in every junction of the event the dataset PSIs lay within a range '
        '(maximum minus minimum) of at most 0.05. Differentially spliced: the ANOVA was significant after Benjamini-'
        'Hochberg correction over the testable events of the cell type (adjusted P ≤ 0.05), and in at least one '
        'junction of the event (i) the difference between the mean PSI of the three human datasets and the mean PSI of '
        'the three mouse datasets was at least 0.1, and (ii) the smallest absolute difference between a human and a '
        'mouse dataset PSI (over the nine human-mouse dataset pairs) was more than 1.5 times the largest absolute '
        'difference between two datasets of the same species (over the three human-human and the three mouse-mouse '
        'pairs). The last criterion requires that every human dataset differs from every mouse dataset more than any '
        'two datasets of the same species, so that the difference is not explained by dataset differences. To test '
        'whether the classification separates species from dataset differences, the same rule was applied to each of '
        'the nine other splits of the six datasets into two groups of three, which mix the species, with the two groups'
        ' in place of the two species (Table S6). All other testable events were considered inconclusive. The usage of '
        'the minor isoform of an event was defined as the PSI of the less used isoform (for skipped exons, the smaller '
        'of the inclusion and exclusion fractions), averaged over the human and mouse datasets (Table S6). As there is '
        'a single fibroblast dataset in each species, this classification cannot be applied to fibroblasts; they were '
        'classified by the status of the single human-mouse fibroblast comparison, as described above.'),
    45: ('Functional enrichment analyses were performed using gseapy enrich module (45) with three MSigDB (46) gene set '
        'collections: GO_Biological_Process_2025 (c5.go.bp), Reactome_2022 (c2.cp.reactome) and Hallmark (h.all; '
        'MSigDB) version v2024.1.Hs, with a background of the 2,590 genes that contain at least one event testable in '
        'at least one immune cell type. Two cell-type-independent gene lists were defined by pooling the '
        'classifications of all events of a gene across the five immune cell types: genes with at least one '
        'differentially spliced classification and no splicing conserved classification (n = 417), and genes with at '
        'least one splicing conserved classification and no differentially spliced classification (n = 295). Enrichment'
        ' was tested for each of the lists against each of the three gene-set libraries. Benjamini–Hochberg False '
        'Discovery Rate (FDR) correction (47) was applied per annotation source. Enrichment results were considered '
        'statistically significant at FDR ≤ 0.1.'),
    49: ('There are 16,092 high confidence human-mouse one-to-one orthologous genes in Ensembl 116, out of which 15,203 '
        'have at least two exons in each species, and 14,769 have at least two orthologous exons. Considering all exons'
        ' from all the transcripts of each orthologous gene, those genes in human include 804,930 unique exons, out of '
        'which 544,278 exons (68%) have mouse orthologs. In mouse, those genes contain 666,955 unique exons, out of '
        'which 494,647 exons (74%) have human orthologs. The lower fraction of human exons with mouse orthologs is due '
        'to the larger number of annotated exons in human (48, 49). There are 390,686 unique orthologous exons '
        'boundaries with orthologous exons defined by at least 50% identity. If the identity threshold is increased to '
        'at least 70% or 80% identity, 354,994 and 302,556 boundaries remain, respectively. If a reciprocal best match '
        'is required, 392,108 boundaries are paired (390,630 of them also with 50% identity); the number is slightly '
        'higher because removing the non-reciprocal exon pairs leaves some boundaries without a competing candidate in '
        'the mutual-best pairing of boundaries (Methods 2.1) (Table S1). Although exon rank was not used to define exon'
        ' orthology, the orthologous exon boundaries used by the alternative splicing events were consistently ordered:'
        ' of the 21,222 boundaries in exons of the canonical transcripts of both species, 86% had the same exon rank in'
        ' human and mouse, and 97% had a constant rank offset within the gene, for example mouse exon n+1 for human '
        'exon n (Table S1, sheet rank_consistency).'),
    50: ('We have used three human and three mouse RNA-seq datasets for each immune cell type: B cells, CD4 T cells, CD8'
        ' T cells, natural killer cells, and monocytes (37, 38, {calderon}), later termed B, T4, T8, NK and Mo, '
        'respectively, to compare alternative splicing use ratio between human and mouse in the immune system (Figure '
        '1B, Tables S4, S2).'),
    51: ('There are 288,674 orthologous junctions that are covered by RNA-seq reads in the immune cell datasets. From '
        'those junctions, 108,612 (38%) are covered in at least two human and two mouse datasets, and by at least 10 '
        'reads in at least two samples in at least two datasets. Most of those junctions do not represent alternative '
        'splicing events. However, 20,205 junctions (19%) form 6,784 alternative splicing events for which more than '
        'one transcript is expressed in human and mouse immune cells (Table S8). Those 6,784 alternative splicing '
        "events are mapped to 4,175 genes and include 3,463 skipped exons, 1,160 alternative 3' splice sites, 651 "
        "alternative 5' splice sites, 251 alternative first exons, 116 alternative last exons, and 1,143 complex events"
        ' that do not fall into one of the above categories (Table S8). In 4,504 of the 5,274 skipped exon and '
        'alternative splice site events (85%), the splice-site correspondence is well supported, that is, the skipped '
        'exon length or the distance between the alternative splice sites is identical in human and mouse.'),
    52: ('The alternative splicing events were tested in each immune cell type for differential splicing between the '
        'three human datasets, GSE60424 (H1), GSE115736 (H2) and GSE118165 (H3) (three human-human pairs), and between '
        'the three mouse datasets, M1, M2 and M3, each composed of one study per cell type (three mouse-mouse pairs) '
        '(Figure S1A), and classified in each comparison as not testable, inconclusive, splicing conserved or '
        'differentially spliced (Figure S1C, Figures 2A, B, Table S9).'),
    54: ('3.2     For a few percent of the testable alternative splicing events, the use ratio differs between human and'
        ' mouse more than between any datasets of the same species'),
    55: ('In the Human-Human comparisons of all immune cell types, on average 25-31% of the testable alternative '
        'splicing events were differentially spliced, and in the Mouse-Mouse comparisons, 18-38% (Table 1, Figures 2A, '
        'B). Human-mouse differences were larger than the differences between datasets of the same species (43-50% '
        'differentially spliced, mean over the nine human-mouse pairs; Figure 2C). This result is independent of '
        'significance thresholds: restricting the analysis to the 2,329-2,887 events per cell type that were testable '
        'in all 15 comparisons, the max(|ΔPSI|) of an event was larger between species than within species (paired '
        'Wilcoxon test, P < 10^-216 in every cell type; Figure 3A, in which each dataset pair is shown separately), and'
        ' junction PSI values were less correlated between human and mouse (Pearson r = 0.80-0.92) than between '
        'datasets of the same species (r = 0.90-0.99; Figures 3B, D, Table S6).'),
    56: ('The relatively high fraction of within species differential splicing in human (21-37%) and mouse (5-40%) '
        'immune cells (Table 1) suggests that the detection of differential splicing is affected by multiple '
        'experimental variables, for example by the cell extraction and sorting protocols, and the sequencing '
        'technology. Moreover, failure to detect a significant difference does not demonstrate that the use ratio is '
        'the same: within species, splicing conservation (LeafCutter P > 0.1 and max(|ΔPSI|) ≤ 0.05) could be '
        'demonstrated on average for only 18-33% of the testable events, and most of the remaining events were '
        'inconclusive (Figures 2A, B, Table S9). As a comparison of a single human and a single mouse dataset (Figure '
        '2C) cannot separate species differences from dataset differences, we classified each event in each cell type '
        'based on the PSI values in all six datasets (Methods 2.5, Figure 1B): 4,069 events in 2,590 genes had a PSI in'
        ' at least two samples of each of the three human and three mouse datasets, and were therefore testable between'
        ' species, in at least one cell type. An event was classified as splicing conserved if the ANOVA over the six '
        'datasets was not significant (P > 0.1) and its PSI varied (maximum minus minimum) by at most 0.05 across all '
        'six datasets, and as differentially spliced if the ANOVA was significant and, in at least one junction, the '
        'mean human and mean mouse PSI differed by at least 0.1 and every one of the nine human-mouse dataset '
        'differences was more than 1.5 times as large as every one of the six differences between datasets of the same '
        'species. Out of the 2,509-2,972 events that were testable in each cell type, 136-232 (5.2-7.8%) were '
        'differentially spliced and 150-221 (5.7-7.8%) were splicing conserved (Figures 2D, S2, Table 1, Tables S10, '
        'S11). The requirement that the species difference exceeds all dataset differences is what separates species '
        'from datasets: when the six datasets were split into two groups of three that mix the species, a difference of'
        ' at least 0.1 between the group means alone was found, on average over the nine such splits, for 55-65% as '
        'many events as for the human-mouse split, but with the requirement only for 2.9-4.9 events per cell type on '
        'average (0-11 per split, 1.5-3.6% of the 136-232 events of the human-mouse split; Table S6). For the '
        'differentially spliced events, the largest difference between datasets of the same species was much smaller '
        'than the smallest human-mouse difference (median 0.08-0.10 vs 0.21-0.26), whereas the splicing conserved '
        'events differed little, as required by their definition, between all datasets (median range 0.02; Figure S3, '
        'Table S11). Nevertheless, counting each event separately in each immune cell type in which it was classified, '
        '63% of the differentially spliced human-mouse classifications were differentially spliced, by a smaller ΔPSI, '
        'also in at least one within-species pair. An example is exon 5 skipping in PUF60 (Figure S4), which is '
        'differentially spliced in CD8 T cells, B cells and monocytes, in which the skipping junction is used more in '
        'all three mouse datasets than in all three human datasets, and inconclusive in CD4 T and NK cells, in which '
        'one dataset (H3 in CD4 T cells) or the differences between datasets of the same species are too large.'),
    58: ('3.3     Human-mouse differential splicing events shared between immune cell types'),
    59: ('Of the 526 events that are differentially spliced in at least one immune cell type, 228 (43%) are '
        'differentially spliced in more than one immune cell type, and 14 in all five (Figure 2E, Table S11): seven '
        "skipped exons, six complex events and an alternative 5' splice site, in HBP1, IFRD1, NFE2L2, PJA2, PSMD4, PTS,"
        " RNF114, RPS24, RSRC2, TAX1BP1, TMSB4X, TRAF3IP3, TXNIP and ZFAND6. The alternative 3' splice site in RPL22L1 "
        'is differentially spliced in three cell types (Figure S5). In the other cell types, the 298 events that are '
        'differentially spliced in only one cell type were not testable in all datasets (47%) or inconclusive (53%), '
        'and only one was splicing conserved (Table S6). Thus, differential splicing in a single cell type mostly '
        'reflects coverage and the stringency of the classification rather than cell type specificity. An example is an'
        " alternative 3' splice site in USP16 (Figure 4), which is differentially spliced in B cells, inconclusive in "
        'CD8 T cells and not testable in CD4 T cells, NK cells and monocytes. The genes containing only differentially '
        'spliced events (pooled over the five immune cell types) are not enriched for any functional term. The '
        'classification was robust to the definition of exon orthology: with at least 70% and 80% exon identity and '
        'with reciprocal best matches, 486, 384 and 532 events were differentially spliced in at least one cell type '
        '(compared with 526 at 50% identity), and in each cell type 5.1-7.8%, 4.8-7.4% and 5.3-7.9% of the testable '
        'events, compared with 5.2-7.8% (Table S6); with events with well-supported splice-site correspondence, '
        '4.4-6.4% of the testable events were differentially spliced (Table S6).'),
    61: ('3.4     Splicing conservation is found mostly in events dominated by one isoform'),
    62: ('There are 390 alternative splicing events that are splicing conserved between human and mouse in at least one '
        'cell type (Table S11). Out of those, 56, 32, 31, 23 and 15 events are splicing conserved only in monocytes, '
        'CD8 T cells, CD4 T cells, B cells and NK cells, respectively. There are 25 alternative splicing events that '
        "are splicing conserved across the five immune cell types, 18 of them alternative 3' splice sites, two in the "
        'ribosomal protein genes RPS3A and RPS5, and others in genes of the translation machinery (EEF1A1, EIF4B, EIF6)'
        ' and of RNA processing (RALY, SRSF11) (Figures 2F, G). Splicing conserved events are dominated by one isoform '
        '– the junction with the lower number of reads accounted for less than 5% of the reads in 99% of the 869 '
        'splicing conserved classifications (each event counted in each cell type in which it was splicing conserved), '
        'which are 6% of all 13,528 testable classifications. Conversely, of the 7,300 testable classifications in '
        'which the minor isoform accounted for at least 5%, only 0.2% were splicing conserved, and 11.4% were '
        'differentially spliced, compared with 1.5% of the classifications in which the minor isoform accounted for '
        'less than 5% (Figure 3C, Table S6). Thus, while species differences are found mostly among events in which '
        'both isoforms are used, splicing conservation was found almost only where one isoform dominates. The '
        "alternative 3' splice site of exon 7 of EIF4B, encoding the translation initiation factor eIF4B (Figure 5), is"
        ' a typical splicing conserved event: the downstream acceptor, two nucleotides from the major one, is used in '
        'less than 1% of the JSRs of the event in almost all samples of both species, and the event is splicing '
        'conserved in all five immune cell types and in fibroblasts.'),
    63: ('The list of genes containing splicing conserved events and no differentially spliced events (n = 295) was '
        'significantly enriched for 26 Reactome terms, six GO biological processes and one hallmark gene set (MYC '
        'targets) (FDR ≤ 0.1), related to translation (eukaryotic translation elongation and initiation, cytoplasmic '
        'translation), ribosome biogenesis and RNA metabolism (Table S12), whereas the list of genes containing only '
        'differentially spliced events (n = 417) was enriched only for one hallmark gene set (genes down-regulated by '
        'UV response, FDR = 0.056). The lists were defined per gene, pooling all events and cell types: 52 genes '
        'contain both a differentially spliced and a splicing conserved event and are in neither list.'),
    66: ('Human-mouse differential splicing events can be due to a difference in splicing code between human and mouse, '
        'in which case such events will be found in all cell types that express the gene, or due to a change in '
        'splicing regulation, in which case they may be found only in some cell types. As we found human-mouse '
        'differential splicing events that are shared between immune cell types, we wanted to extend beyond the immune '
        'system. Thus, we compared the human-mouse splicing ratio between immune cells and dermal fibroblasts of human '
        'and mouse that were cultured and sequenced in the same study (Methods 2.2) (40). Out of the 6,784 orthologous '
        'alternative splicing events, 5,671 (84%) were testable between human and mouse fibroblasts; 1,286 (23%) of '
        'them were differentially spliced, 1,569 (28%) were splicing conserved and 2,816 (50%) were inconclusive (Table'
        ' 1). Out of the 119-209 events that were differentially spliced in each immune cell type and testable in '
        'fibroblasts, 50-62% are differentially spliced also in fibroblasts, 7-15% are splicing conserved, and 31-37% '
        'are inconclusive (Figure 6A). Out of the 143-210 events that were splicing conserved in each immune cell type '
        'and testable in fibroblasts, 31-33% are splicing conserved also in fibroblasts and only 0-3% are '
        'differentially spliced (Figure 6B). Conversely, out of the 324-402 events differentially spliced in '
        'fibroblasts that are testable in each immune cell type, 20-29% are differentially spliced in the immune cell '
        'type and only 0-1% splicing conserved (Figure 6C), and out of the 633-725 splicing conserved events in '
        'fibroblasts, 2-4% are differentially spliced and 7-9% splicing conserved in immune cell types (Figure 6D); '
        'most of the other events are inconclusive. Events that were differentially spliced in more immune cell types '
        'were more often differentially spliced also in fibroblasts (38 of the 47 events differentially spliced in four'
        ' or five immune cell types and testable in fibroblasts, compared with 39% of the events differentially spliced'
        ' in one immune cell type and 13% of the events not differentially spliced in any immune cell type; Table S11),'
        ' and none of the 25 events splicing conserved in all five immune cell types was differentially spliced in '
        'fibroblasts (8 splicing conserved, 17 inconclusive).'),
    69: ('Here, we performed a systematic comparison of alternative splicing usage between human and mouse across five '
        'immune cell types, each profiled in three human and three mouse RNA-seq datasets. We identified 6,784 '
        'orthologous alternative splicing events that are expressed in immune cells; in each cell type, 5.7-7.8% of the'
        ' testable events were splicing conserved, mostly events in which one isoform accounts for more than 95% of the'
        ' reads, 5.2-7.8% were differentially spliced between species by at least 0.1 and by more than every difference'
        ' between datasets of the same species, and the remaining 85-89% were inconclusive. Thus, even when a gene and '
        'its exons are orthologous and the junctions of an alternative splicing event are expressed in both species, '
        'that is, when the gene sequence, its expression and its alternative splicing repertoire are conserved, '
        'splicing conservation could rarely be demonstrated for events in which both isoforms are used, and for some '
        'events the use ratio differs between species more than between datasets of the same species, with implications'
        ' for how mouse models are interpreted in immunological research. The robust differences in immune cells were '
        'often present also in fibroblasts.'),
    70: ('The splicing differences between human and mouse datasets were larger, and the PSI values less correlated, '
        'than between datasets of the same species. While this indicates that the human-mouse difference in splicing '
        'ratio is not merely due to technical differences between RNA-seq datasets, the within-species comparisons also'
        ' showed that many events change due to dataset differences (21-37% and 5-40% differentially spliced within '
        'human and mouse). Thus, we based the interpretation of species divergence on events in which the ANOVA over '
        'the six datasets was significant, the mean human and mouse PSI differed by at least 0.1, and every human '
        'dataset differed from every mouse dataset by more than 1.5 times the largest difference between datasets of '
        'the same species: 136-232 per cell type (526 events in total). A difference between the mean human and mean '
        'mouse PSI alone also reflects differences between laboratories and protocols: when the datasets were split '
        'into two groups that mix the species, such a difference was found for 55-65% as many events as for the species'
        ' split, whereas with the requirement that every between-group difference exceeds 1.5 times every within-group '
        'difference, for 1.5-3.6% as many. Many of the differentially spliced events (63% of the classifications) were '
        'differentially spliced, by a smaller ΔPSI, also between datasets of the same species. For most events, the '
        'dataset differences are too large to determine whether the use ratio is conserved: 85-89% of the testable '
        'events in each cell type were inconclusive, so that the fraction of differentially spliced events (5-8%) '
        'reflects the stringency of the classification rather than the fraction of events whose use ratio differs '
        'between species. Among the classified events, the numbers of splicing conserved and differentially spliced '
        'events were similar (0.7-1.2-fold; Table 1), but among the events in which the minor isoform accounts for at '
        'least 5% of the reads, almost all classified events were differentially spliced (11.4% vs 0.2% splicing '
        'conserved). The consistency of the results with stricter definitions of exon orthology and with events with '
        'well-supported splice-site correspondence (Table S6) argues that the species difference is not an artifact of '
        'the orthology assignment.'),
    71: ('Of the 526 events that are differentially spliced between human and mouse in at least one immune cell type, 14'
        " are shared between all five cell types: seven skipped exons, six complex events and an alternative 5' splice "
        "site, for example in NFE2L2, PSMD4, RPS24, TXNIP and TRAF3IP3. The alternative 3' splice sites of RPL22L1 "
        '(Figure S5), which is differentially spliced in three immune cell types, and of USP16 (Figure 4), which is '
        'differentially spliced in B cells, are three nucleotides apart. In USP16, a histone H2A deubiquitinase, the '
        'three additional nucleotides code for an additional Alanine at the beginning of exon 6, upstream of Cysteine '
        '205 that is required for USP16 deubiquitinase activity (50). In the ribosome-associated protein RPL22L1 '
        '(Figure S5), the three additional nucleotides add Glutamine. Whether these differences affect protein function'
        ' remains to be tested. Genes containing NAGNAG events, that is, AG dinucleotides that are separated by one '
        'nucleotide, are conserved between human and mouse and were found to be enriched for DNA binding genes (51). In'
        " USP16, the shorter exon was more frequent in human immune cells. Testing all testable alternative 3' and 5' "
        'splice site events, events with alternative splice sites up to six nucleotides apart were not more often '
        "differentially spliced between species than events with more distant splice sites (alternative 3' splice "
        "sites: 5.5% vs 4.1%, Fisher's exact test, P = 0.20; alternative 5' splice sites: 6.8% vs 6.8%, P = 1.0; Table "
        'S6).'),
    72: ('Splicing conservation between human and mouse could be demonstrated for 390 events in 347 genes, almost all of'
        ' which are events in which one isoform accounts for more than 95% of the reads. The genes carrying the '
        'splicing conserved events were enriched for translation, ribosome biogenesis and RNA metabolism, partly due to'
        " ribosomal proteins. An example is the alternative 3' splice site of exon 7 of the translation initiation "
        'factor EIF4B (Figure 5), in which the minor acceptor is used in less than 1% of the reads in both species. '
        'Genes encoding core RNA-processing and splicing machinery are generally among the most evolutionarily '
        'constrained genes in mammals, in terms of sequence ({lek}) and expression level ({brawand}). This is '
        'consistent with stronger purifying selection and intolerance to loss of function compared to genes involved in'
        ' more peripheral or context-dependent signaling pathways, such as those in immune and inflammatory response, '
        'which show greater divergence in both sequence and expression across mammals (53). It is possible that those '
        'genes are also under stronger purifying selection for splice ratio, which would predict splicing ratio '
        'conservation in all tissues and cell types. However, this hypothesis remains to be tested, as the splicing '
        'conserved events are mostly dominated by one isoform, for which a similar use ratio is easier to demonstrate.'),
    73: ('To test if indeed the human-mouse conserved splicing ratio in the immune system is also conserved in other '
        'cell types, we compared immune cell types to fibroblasts, a purified non-immune cell type. Out of the events '
        'splicing conserved in each immune cell type, 31-33% were splicing conserved also in fibroblasts and only 0-3% '
        'differentially spliced (Figure 6B); the others were inconclusive in fibroblasts, as the single fibroblast '
        'comparison has less power than the six immune datasets. Out of the events differentially spliced in each '
        'immune cell type, 50-62% were differentially spliced also in fibroblasts (Figure 6A). An example of an event '
        'that differs between species in immune cells but is not differentially spliced in fibroblasts is the skipped '
        'exon in UCKL1, which is skipped often in mouse immune cells, but is hardly skipped in human immune cells '
        '(Figure S6). Events that are differentially spliced in more immune cell types were more often differentially '
        'spliced also in fibroblasts (81% of the events differentially spliced in four or five immune cell types, '
        'compared with 39% of the events differentially spliced in one immune cell type and 13% of the events not '
        'differentially spliced in any immune cell type), for example RPL22L1 (Figure S5), USP16 (Figure 4) and PUF60 '
        '(Figure S4) are differentially spliced also in fibroblasts. Thus, robust human-mouse differences are often '
        'shared between cell types, and may reflect changes in cis-regulatory sequence or splicing code, while other '
        'differences may reflect immune-specific regulation. As only one human and one mouse fibroblast dataset were '
        'analyzed, the fibroblasts were cultured whereas the immune cells were sorted ex vivo, and the fibroblasts were'
        ' classified by a single dataset pair, these comparisons are supportive rather than definitive evidence for '
        'cell type specificity.'),
    77: ('Whereas most human exons have mouse orthologs, and most human genes have more than one transcript, only 6,784 '
        'alternative splicing events were covered by enough RNA-seq reads to be compared between the species. This '
        'means that in order to accurately compare splicing ratio between species, mapping of orthologous alternative '
        'splicing events between species should be done per cell type.'),
    78: ('The ability to identify testable orthologous alternative splicing events is limited to relatively highly '
        'expressed genes, as it requires a minimal coverage of the splicing junctions by RNA-seq reads. Thus, only '
        '4,069 alternative splicing events, within 2,590 genes, were testable in at least one cell type, and our '
        'conclusions refer to such sufficiently expressed and testable orthologous alternative splicing events in the '
        'analyzed datasets rather than to the entire human-mouse alternative splicing landscape. Of these genes, 1,613 '
        'have a single testable event, whereas 977 carry 2-9 testable orthologous alternative splicing events, which '
        'often display different statuses. For example, SRSF7 carries four orthologous alternative splicing events – a '
        "skipped exon, an alternative 5' splice site, an alternative 3' splice site and a complex event; the "
        "alternative 5' splice site is differentially spliced in four immune cell types and in fibroblasts, whereas the"
        ' other three events are inconclusive in all five. Much deeper sequencing, and more datasets per species, are '
        'required to compare splicing ratio of most orthologous alternative splicing events.'),
    80: ('Several technical and biological factors may have influenced both the number and identity of the detected '
        'differentially spliced and splicing conserved alternative splicing events between human and mouse orthologous '
        'genes. First, sequencing depth directly affects the number of testable alternative splicing events between and'
        ' within species. Second, the number of biological replicates affect statistical power, and in some datasets '
        'and cell types there were only three or four replicates. Third, with three human and three mouse datasets, the'
        ' species difference cannot be tested at the level of datasets with useful power; the ANOVA uses the samples of'
        ' the datasets as replicates, and the requirement that the human-mouse difference is at least 0.1 and that '
        'every human-mouse dataset difference exceeds 1.5 times every within-species difference is a threshold rather '
        'than a statistical test of the dataset effect, although very few events met it when the datasets were split '
        'into groups that mix the species. Fourth, sequencing-related biases may differ between datasets due to '
        'differences in library preparation protocols (polyA, total RNA and Smart-seq2), read quality, read length, and'
        ' alignment efficiency. Fifth, biological heterogeneity also represents an important source of variation. Human'
        ' samples inherently contain substantial genetic variation between donors, which may increase inter-individual '
        'variability in splicing patterns compared to inbred mouse strains. Furthermore, some annotated cell '
        'populations, such as total CD4 and CD8 T cells in H1 and H2 and total CD8 T cells in M1, contain distinct '
        'cellular subpopulations with unique transcriptional and splicing programs, potentially masking or diluting '
        'species-shared splicing signatures. Additional factors such as differences in sampled tissues (peripheral '
        'blood in human; spleen, lymph nodes, bone marrow and blood in mouse), developmental stage/age (adults in '
        'human, young adult mice where the age is given), sex composition and mouse genetic background (C57BL/6, B6129,'
        ' or not given) (Table S2) may further contribute to species-specific splicing patterns and complicate the '
        'interpretation of similar versus different use ratio of alternative splicing events. However, comparisons '
        'within each species that differed only in sex, age or tissue of origin showed far smaller splicing differences'
        ' than the human-mouse comparisons, and even smaller than those between datasets of the same species '
        '(Supplementary Note 1, Figure S7), indicating that these factors do not explain the human-mouse differences. '
        'Sixth, RNA-sequencing displays sequence dependent coverage bias (62). This bias is canceled out for within '
        'species differential splicing, but between species comparison of splicing junctions spanned by different '
        'sequences with different bias may inflate the estimate of differentially spliced events.'),
    81: ('To partially account for some of those sources of variability and improve the robustness of our analysis, we '
        'used three independent datasets per species, and required events that are differentially spliced between '
        'species to differ significantly between the datasets (ANOVA), with a difference of at least 0.1 between the '
        'mean human and mouse PSI and every human-mouse dataset difference more than 1.5 times as large as every '
        'difference between datasets of the same species. Splicing conserved events were defined by the absence of a '
        'difference between the six datasets (ANOVA P > 0.1) together with a PSI range of at most 0.05, rather than by '
        'the absence of a significant human-mouse difference alone. The remaining events, which were inconclusive, '
        'differed between datasets of the same species too much, differed between species less than the dataset '
        'differences, or could not be tested in all datasets; they may be more sensitive to technical variability, '
        'sample heterogeneity, or context-specific regulation.'),
    87: ('Publicly available datasets were analyzed in this study. This data can be found here: '
        'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/query/acc.cgi?acc=GSE60424, '
        'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/query/acc.cgi?acc=GSE115736, '
        'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/query/acc.cgi?acc=GSE118165, '
        'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/query/acc.cgi?acc=GSE287527, '
        'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/query/acc.cgi?acc=GSE215775, '
        'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/query/acc.cgi?acc=GSE313371, '
        'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/query/acc.cgi?acc=GSE261448, '
        'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/query/acc.cgi?acc=GSE307667, '
        'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/query/acc.cgi?acc=GSE184496, '
        'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/query/acc.cgi?acc=GSE263132, '
        'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/query/acc.cgi?acc=GSE236819, '
        'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/query/acc.cgi?acc=GSE254202, '
        'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/query/acc.cgi?acc=GSE231772, '
        'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/query/acc.cgi?acc=GSE134322, '
        'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/query/acc.cgi?acc=GSE284441, '
        'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/query/acc.cgi?acc=GSE180053, '
        'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/bioproject/PRJNA797236, '
        'https://www.ebi.ac.uk/biostudies/arrayexpress/studies/E-MTAB-5919, '
        'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/query/acc.cgi?acc=GSE116177, '
        'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/query/acc.cgi?acc=GSE107011, '
        'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/query/acc.cgi?acc=GSE143213, '
        'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/query/acc.cgi?acc=GSE133383, '
        'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/query/acc.cgi?acc=GSE233347, '
        'https://www.encodeproject.org/experiments/ENCSR703ACF/ (and ENCSR788PEE, ENCSR243UCH, ENCSR092BBW).'),
    222: ('Figure 1 - Outline of the human-mouse splicing ratio comparative analysis. A. Definition of orthology for '
        'exons, junctions and alternative splicing events for all orthologous genes was performed. Shown is an '
        'orthologous skipped exon event in human and mouse. Exons are marked by black boxes, and orthologous exons are '
        'connected by dashed lines. Splicing junctions are represented as arcs between exons, and orthologous splicing '
        'junctions are in the same color. B. The human-mouse classification of each orthologous alternative splicing '
        'event in each immune cell type, based on the PSI in the three human (H1-H3) and three mouse (M1-M3, one study '
        'per cell type) datasets: an event is testable if it has a PSI in at least two samples of each dataset; '
        'splicing conserved if the ANOVA over the six datasets (on the junction with the largest range of dataset PSIs)'
        ' is not significant (P > 0.1) and the dataset PSIs vary by at most 0.05 in every junction; differentially '
        'spliced if the ANOVA is significant (adjusted P ≤ 0.05) and, in at least one junction, the mean human and mean'
        ' mouse PSI differ by at least 0.1 and the smallest |ΔPSI| between a human and a mouse dataset is more than 1.5'
        ' times the largest |ΔPSI| between two datasets of the same species; and inconclusive otherwise. The single '
        'dataset pairs, fibroblast and sex, age and tissue comparisons are shown in Figure S1.'),
    224: ('Figure 2 - Classification of human-mouse orthologous alternative splicing events based on the conservation of '
        'their use ratio. Stacked bars display the percentages of testable orthologous alternative splicing events '
        'which are differentially spliced, splicing conserved or inconclusive in the (A) human-human (three dataset '
        'pairs), (B) mouse-mouse (three dataset pairs) and (C) human-mouse (nine dataset pairs) comparisons, in which '
        'each dataset pair is tested separately (Figure S1C), and between human and mouse fibroblasts (Fb, a single '
        'dataset pair). Each percentage is the mean over the dataset pairs; the values of each pair are in Table S9. D.'
        ' Human-mouse classification of the alternative splicing events from all six datasets (Methods 2.5, Figure 1B):'
        ' percentage of testable events that are differentially spliced or splicing conserved. E. UpSet plot of the '
        'differentially spliced events shared between cell types. Only the 25 most frequent combinations of cell types '
        'are shown, covering 511 of the 526 events; differential splicing in a single cell type does not imply cell '
        'type specificity, as in the other cell types most of these events are not testable in all datasets or are '
        'inconclusive (Table S6). F. UpSet plot of the splicing conserved events. Only the 25 most frequent '
        'combinations are shown, covering 364 of the 390 events. G. Heatmap of the 249 events that are differentially '
        'spliced (green) or splicing conserved (blue) in at least three immune cell types, colored by their class in '
        'each immune cell type, and their status in fibroblasts (right bar). Fb – fibroblasts.'),
    226: ('Figure 3 - The amplitude of human-mouse splicing differences is larger than that of within-species '
        'differences. A. Cumulative distribution of the max(|ΔPSI|) of the events testable in all 15 comparisons of '
        'each cell type (n, above each panel); each line is one dataset pair. Dotted line, ΔPSI = 0.1. B. Pearson '
        'correlation of junction PSI between the two datasets of each pair, on the same events as in A; each dot is one'
        ' dataset pair. C. Percentage of the testable classifications (each event in each immune cell type) that are '
        'differentially spliced or splicing conserved between human and mouse (Figure 1B), by the usage of the minor '
        'isoform (the PSI of the less used isoform, averaged over the datasets). D. Density of the PSI of the testable '
        'junctions in NK cells (hexagonal bins; darker, more junctions) for one human-human (H1 vs H2), mouse-mouse (M1'
        ' vs M2) and human-mouse (H2 vs M1) comparison; line, identity; r, Pearson correlation coefficient. A, B, D: '
        'blue, human-human; orange, human-mouse; green, mouse-mouse.'),
    228: ("Figure 4 - The orthologous alternative 3' splice site event in USP16 is differentially spliced between human "
        "and mouse B cells. A. The event at the 3' end of intron 5 in human (top) and mouse (bottom). Exons are shown "
        'with their nucleotide sequence and the encoded amino acids of the canonical and the alternative transcripts; '
        'arcs are the two junctions, with their coordinates on the right. The canonical (dark blue) junction includes '
        'the three nucleotides marked in yellow (CAG), which code for an additional alanine (position 150 of the human '
        'canonical protein) compared with the alternative (light blue) junction. B. Junction spanning reads (JSR) of '
        'the two junctions in each sample (top) and the same counts normalized to PSI (bottom), grouped by cell type '
        'and, within each cell type, by dataset (dashed lines). Colors are as in A. M3, M2, M1 – mouse datasets; H1, '
        'H2, H3 – human datasets; M, H – mouse and human fibroblasts; T4, T8 – CD4 and CD8 T cells; B – B cells; NK – '
        'NK cells; Mo – monocytes.'),
    230: ("Figure 5 - The orthologous alternative 3' splice site event of EIF4B exon 7 is splicing conserved between "
        'human and mouse. A. The event in human (top) and mouse (bottom): the two acceptors of exon 7 are two '
        'nucleotides apart in both species; the hatched part is the two nucleotides included only by the dark blue '
        'junction, which is drawn below the exons; exon labels, rank in the canonical transcript and coding length; '
        'introns are not to scale. B. Stacked bars display the number of junction spanning reads that cover the '
        'alternative splicing junctions in each sample (top) and the same numbers normalized to the total to display '
        'PSI values (bottom). Colors are as in A. M3, M2, M1 – mouse datasets; H1, H2, H3 – human datasets; M, H – '
        'mouse and human fibroblasts.'),
}

INSERT_UPDATES6 = {
    29: [(29, ('Raw sequencing reads of the datasets used were downloaded from SRA using the NCBI SRA Toolkit (v3.0.0, '
        'Available from: https://github.com/ncbi/sra-tools). Reads of GSE118165 and of the mouse immune datasets were '
        'trimmed with the same cutadapt (v2.6) ({cutadapt}) command for all samples, which removes Nextera and TruSeq '
        "adapter sequences from the 3' end and the Smart-seq2 ISPCR oligonucleotide from the 5' end of each read (all "
        'three in every sample), low quality ends (quality < 20) and reads shorter than 30 nucleotides. For GSE236819, '
        'whose runs contain a 76-nucleotide read and an index read, only the 76-nucleotide read was used. Hisat2 (42) '
        '(version 2.0.5) was used to align human or mouse RNA-seq reads to the Homo sapiens genome (GRCh38) or Mus '
        'musculus genome (GRCm39), respectively. The resulting binary alignment map (BAM) files were sorted and indexed'
        ' by SAMtools (43). To evaluate the quality of the data, the flagstat option of SAMtools was used. All samples '
        'from all datasets had at least 12 million mapped reads and more than 76% of the reads were aligned (Tables S2,'
        ' S4). All samples were used for the analysis.')), (29, ('A dataset was used if it contained bulk RNA-seq of sorted or purified cells of the cell type, from healthy '
        'donors (unstimulated cells) or from unmanipulated wild-type mice or the control group of a genetically '
        'modified mouse line (flox-only or Cre-only mice), without treatment or infection, with at least three '
        'biological replicates of the cell type and with public raw reads; human datasets were also required to profile'
        ' all five cell types in the same donors. Among the datasets that met these criteria, naive or resting cells, '
        'more replicates, longer and paired-end reads and deeper sequencing were preferred, and the datasets of each '
        'cell type were taken from different studies. The human datasets, with four or five replicates of each cell '
        'type, are GSE60424 (38), termed H1 (healthy control samples only), GSE115736 (37), termed H2, and GSE118165 '
        '({calderon}), termed H3 (unstimulated samples only). The three mouse datasets of each cell type, termed M1, M2'
        ' and M3 (Figure 1B), with three to six replicates, are: B cells – follicular B cells from GSE287527 (M1), from'
        ' Mb1-Cre control mice of GSE307667 (M2) and from Bcl6 flox control mice of GSE231772 (M3); CD4 T cells – naive'
        ' CD4 T cells from GSE215775 (M1), from the young mice of GSE184496 (M2) and from the wild-type littermates of '
        'GSE134322 (M3); CD8 T cells – CD8 T cells of uninfected wild-type mice from GSE313371 (M1) and naive CD8 T '
        'cells from GSE263132 (M2) and GSE284441 (M3); NK cells – spleen NK cells of phosphate-buffered saline (PBS) '
        'treated control mice from GSE261448 (M1), of wild-type mice from GSE236819 (M2) and of Mettl3 flox control '
        'mice from GSE180053 (M3); monocytes – Ly6C+ bone marrow monocytes of PBS-treated control mice from GSE261448 '
        '(M1), Ly6Chi spleen monocytes from GSE254202 (M2) and Ly6Chi blood monocytes from PRJNA797236 (M3). Human and '
        'mouse dermal fibroblasts were taken from E-MTAB-5919 (40), which includes both species. The samples used, '
        'their strain, tissue source, sorting markers, cell state, treatment and replicate identity are detailed in '
        'Table S2.'))],
    231: [(230, ('Figure 6 – Comparison of human-mouse splicing ratio differences between the immune system and fibroblasts. (A,'
        ' B) Distribution of the status in fibroblasts of alternative splicing events that are (A) differentially '
        'spliced or (B) splicing conserved in each immune cell type. (C, D) Distribution of the classification in each '
        'immune cell type of alternative splicing events that are (C) differentially spliced or (D) splicing conserved '
        'between human and mouse fibroblasts. Number above each bar: the number of alternative splicing events that are'
        ' testable both in fibroblasts and in the immune cell type, which is the denominator of the percentages of that'
        ' bar; it differs between bars because each class and cell type contains a different number of events.')), (231, '')],
}

SUPP_NOTE_1 = [
    ('To test whether differences in sex, age or tissue of origin between the human and mouse samples could explain '
        'the human-mouse differences, we compared, separately in each dataset, between samples of the same dataset '
        '(species, laboratory and protocol) and cell type that differ in a single factor (sex, age or tissue). In '
        'human: male vs female donors of sorted naive B cells, naive CD4 and CD8 T cells, NK cells and classical '
        'monocytes (GSE107011, two vs two donors, the same four donors in all five cell types) (Monaco et al., 2019), '
        'young (23-35 years) vs old (65-78 years) donors of naive CD4 T cells (GSE143213, four vs four), and CD56dim NK'
        ' cells from blood vs spleen of the same three organ donors (GSE133383) (Dogra et al., 2020). In mouse: spleen '
        'B cells of female vs male B6CASTF1/J F1 hybrid mice (ENCODE ENCSR703ACF, ENCSR788PEE, ENCSR243UCH and '
        'ENCSR092BBW, eight vs eight) (ENCODE Project Consortium, 2020; as the females carry a CAST and a B6 X '
        'chromosome and the males only a B6 X chromosome, allelic differences on chromosome X may appear as sex '
        'differences; chromosome X was not excluded, so that this comparison may overestimate the sex differences), '
        'naive CD4 T cells of young (7-11 weeks) vs old (83-86 weeks) mice (GSE184496, six vs six, three of each sex; '
        'the young mice are the M2 CD4 T cells), Ly49H+ NK cells from spleen vs liver of the same mice (GSE233347, '
        'three vs three), monocytes from bone marrow vs blood (GSE116177, two vs two), and Ly6Chi monocytes from blood '
        'vs spleen of male mice (PRJNA797236, four vs four; the blood monocytes are the M3 monocytes). Reads were '
        'processed as for the mouse datasets of the main analysis (Methods 2.2), and junction counts were added for the'
        ' same alternative splicing events.'),
    ('Each control comparison was tested as a single comparison (Figure S1C), on the events testable in the control '
        'comparison and in all nine human-mouse pairs (1,926-2,814 events per comparison). Of these events, including '
        'the inconclusive and splicing conserved ones, only 0-0.15% were differentially spliced between male and female'
        ' (human, five cell types; mouse B cells), 0-1.2% between young and old (human and mouse naive CD4 T cells), '
        'and 0-6.2% between tissues (human NK cells from blood and spleen, mouse NK cells from spleen and liver, and '
        'mouse monocytes from bone marrow and blood and from blood and spleen) (Figure S7A, Table S13). As the control '
        'comparisons had fewer replicates than some of the dataset comparisons, we also compared effect sizes, which do'
        ' not depend on the number of significant calls: the median max(|ΔPSI|) was 0.015-0.045 in the controls vs '
        '0.087-0.118 between human and mouse (mean over the human-mouse dataset pairs), and PSI correlations were '
        '0.98-0.996 vs 0.85-0.90 (Figures S7B, C); the human-mouse differences were larger than those of every control '
        'comparison (paired Wilcoxon test, P < 10^-200). Thus, sex, age and tissue of origin cannot account for the '
        'human-mouse splicing differences; they caused even smaller differences than those between datasets of the same'
        ' species (median max(|ΔPSI|) 0.052-0.089).'),
]
